<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="Slope" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Slope</title>
    <p>
        In this section, we will learn how to calculate and interpret the slope between two points. We will also define a linear function using our understanding of slope.
    </p>


    <definition xml:id="SlopeDefinition">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <term>Slope</term>, <m>m</m>, between two points is determined by the change in output divided by the change in input between the two points. For points <m>(x_1, y_1)</m> and <m>(x_2, y_2)</m>, the slope is given by the formula:
                <me>
                    m = \frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}= \frac{y_1 - y_2}{x_1 - x_2}
                </me>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>


    <p>
      The above equation is showing that the order in which we subtract the y-values and x-values does not matter. However, make sure that if you start with <m>y_2</m> in the numerator, then you also start with <m>x_2</m> in the denominator, and similaryly, if you start with <m>y_1</m> in the numerator, then you also start with <m>x_1</m> in the denominator.
    </p>
    <p>
      We also refer to the slope as the "rise over run" or the "change in y over change in x".
    </p>
    <me>
      m = \frac{\text{Change in }y}{\text{Change in }x} = \frac{\text{Rise}}{\text{Run}}
    </me>


    <example xml:id="example-slope-two-points">
    <title>Slope between Two Points</title>
    <p> 
      What is the slope between the points <m>(4,-3)</m> and <m>(1,3)</m>? 
    </p>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We first decide which point will be <m>(x_1, y_1)</m> and which point will be <m>(x_2, y_2)</m>. This choice is up to you, and is the reason behind the order not mattering as shown in <xref ref="SlopeDefinition"/>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Let's choose <m>(4,-3)</m> to be <m>(x_1, y_1)</m> and <m>(1,3)</m> to be <m>(x_2, y_2)</m>. Then we can substitute into the formula for slope:
        <me>
          m = \frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}=\frac{3 - (-3)}{1 - 4} = \frac{6}{-3} = -2
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        We could also have used the other order and get the same value in the end:
        <me>
          m = \frac{y_1 - y_2}{x_1 - x_2}=\frac{-3 - 3}{4 - 1} = \frac{-6}{3} = -2
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        So, the slope between the points <m>(4,-3)</m> and <m>(1,3)</m> is <m>-2</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-slope-function-notation">
    <title>Slope between Two Points (Function Notation)</title>
    <p>
      Let <m> f(x)=x^2 </m> and <m> g(x)=2x+1 </m>. What is the slope between the points <m>(2,f(2))</m> and <m>(1,g(1))</m>?
    </p>
    <solution>
      <p>
        First, we need to find the coordinates of the two points. We can do this by substituting into the functions:
        <me>
          f(2) = 2^2 = 4
        </me>
        and
        <me>          
          g(1) = 2(1)+1 = 3
        </me>
        So, the two points are <m>(2,4)</m> and <m>(1,3)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now we can use the formula for slope to find the slope between these two points:
        <me>
          m = \frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}=\frac{3 - 4}{1 - 2} = \frac{-1}{-1} = 1
        </me>
      </p>
    </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-slope-rise-run">
    <title>Slope between Two Points (Rise over Run)</title>
    <p>
      What is the slope between the points <m>(1,1)</m> and <m>(4,5)</m>?
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="slope-figure">
      <caption>Graph of the two points</caption>
      <image width="60%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="Slope-Annotated">
          <description> Slope of a line segment between two points on a graph </description>
            <diagram dimensions="(400,300)" margins="5">
              <definition>x1=1</definition>
              <definition>x2=4</definition>
              <definition>y1=1</definition>
              <definition>y2=5</definition>
              <definition>p=(x1,y1)</definition>
              <definition>q=(x2,y2)</definition>
              <definition>r=(x2,y1)</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,6,6)">
              <axes decorations="no" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
              <group outline="none">
              <polygon points="(p,r,q)" stroke="red"/>
              </group>
              <label anchor="((x1+x2)/2,y1)" alignment="south">
                <m>\text{Change in } x</m>
              </label>
              <label anchor="((x1+x2)/2,y1-.5)" alignment="south">
                <m>\text{"Run"}</m>
              </label>
              <label anchor="(x2,(y1+y2)/2)" alignment="east">
                <m>\text{Change in } y</m>
              </label>
              <label anchor="(x2,(y1+y2)/2-.5)" alignment="east">
                <m>\text{"Rise"}</m>
              </label>
              <point at="point1" p="(x1,y1)" alignment="nw">
                <m>(1,1)</m>
              </point>
              <point at="point2" p="(x2,y2)" alignment="nw">
                <m>(4,5)</m>
              </point>
            </coordinates>

              <annotations>
                <annotation ref="figure"
                      text="Graph showing the how the change in y and change in x is found on a graph">
                <annotation ref="point1" text="The point x=1 and y=1"/>
                <annotation ref="point2" text="The point x=4 and y=5"/>
              </annotation>
              </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <solution>
      <p>
        You can find the slope just as we did in example <xref ref="example-slope-two-points"/> by substituting into the formula for slope. However, let's use the alternative definition for this example. Namely, let's find the change in <m>y</m> and the change in <m>x</m> from the graph. 
      </p>
      <p>
        To do this, pick a point to start with. I'll choose <m>(1,1)</m>. Then, to get to <m>(4,5)</m> I must go up 4 units (the change in <m>y</m>) and right <m>3</m> units (the change in <m>x</m>). Note that going up or to the right is positive, while going down or to the left is negative. So, the change in <m>y</m> is <m>4</m> and the change in <m>x</m> is <m>3</m>. Therefore, the slope is:
        <me>
          m = \frac{\text{Change in }y}{\text{Change in }x} = \frac{4}{3}
        </me>
      </p>
    </solution>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="LinearDefinition">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A function is called a <term>Linear Function</term> if the slope is constant. In other words, a function is linear if the slope between any two points is the same.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    
